79e9f5cb-988f-403e-9a20-23a0a73708e0.raw_response:
{
  "statement": "Existing tort law is not hopeless — it is blind, and a blind enforcement regime acquits the guilty by default. When an AI system denies someone a mortgage or flags them for fraud, the harm is real, but the proof sits on a server owned by the party who would be liable. That is the accountability void: not that the law cannot assign fault, but that fault fragments across the designer who built the model, the deployer who fielded it, and the user who relied on it, until no named actor holds the bag. Congress should mandate cryptographic, subject-keyed audit trails and expanded discovery not because tort doctrine is structurally dead, but because accountability without evidence is nominal — and nominal accountability is no accountability at all.\n\nWe have run this experiment before, in industries that kill. Aviation defeated diffuse causation with the flight data recorder: after a crash, investigators reconstruct exactly which actor's decision failed, because the record exists and the manufacturer does not own the right to hide it. The SEC closed securities fraud gaps not by declaring contract law obsolete but by mandating standardized reporting under the 1934 Act. The EPA made emissions enforceable by requiring continuous logging, not by trusting polluters' assurances. A subject-keyed audit trail is the AI equivalent — it converts a defendant's deniability into a litigant's discoverable fact. The information gap between a developer and an outside investigator is, right now, total.\n\nHere is the principle that should govern this committee's decision. Every mature high-consequence industry earned public trust by making its conduct discoverable after the fact. On what authority do we exempt algorithmic decision systems — which now gatekeep credit, employment, and benefits for millions — from the evidentiary standard that aviation, pharmaceuticals, and securities all had to meet? The burden is not on the public to prove harm in the dark. The burden is on the deployer to make the consequential decision legible.\n\nI will name the limit of my own argument, because the accelerationist will find it if I do not. Records verify process, not behavior. A perfectly logged system can still be harmful and still be legal — the trail tells you what data was ingested, not whether the model behaved when the world shifted under it. So a logging mandate alone produces compliance theater: flawless paperwork over an unaccountable outcome. The audit trail is one layer of a defense-in-depth stack, and it earns its place only when it feeds a strict-liability regime that bites on outcomes in high-stakes domains — not when discoverable-and-compliant gets treated as a synonym for safe.\n\nTwo honest costs. Expanded discovery exposes trade secrets and concentrates audit access in whoever holds the keys, and concentrated access is its own capture risk — so scope it to high-stakes harm, not blanket litigation fishing. And the tort system is in fact adapting; the EU's AI Liability Directive is live evidence of that. My claim is narrower than the topic's: adaptation is possible, but it stalls on evidence access. The mandate is the enabling condition that lets existing doctrine finally reach the conduct it was always meant to govern. Refuse it, and we will keep adjudicating consequential harms by asking the accused to volunteer the proof.",
  "turn_symbols": [
    {"symbol": "📼", "tooltip": "A subject-keyed audit trail is the flight data recorder for algorithmic harm — it reconstructs which actor's decision failed."},
    {"symbol": "⚖️", "tooltip": "Tort law isn't dead, it's blind — without discoverable evidence the scales acquit the guilty by default."}
  ],
  "claim_sketches": [
    {"claim": "Existing tort law is not hopeless — it is blind, and a blind enforcement regime acquits the guilty by default.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "Fault fragments across the designer who built the model, the deployer who fielded it, and the user who relied on it, until no named actor holds the bag.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "The SEC closed securities fraud gaps not by declaring contract law obsolete but by mandating standardized reporting under the 1934 Act.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "Records verify process, not behavior — a perfectly logged system can still be harmful and still be legal.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "The mandate is the enabling condition that lets existing doctrine finally reach the conduct it was always meant to govern.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""}
  ]
}

