  "statement": "The Safetyist made the strongest possible case for this mandate, and I want to be fair to it: tort doctrine isn't dead, it's blind. Their argument is that judges can't reach conduct they can't see, that plaintiffs lose not because the law fails but because the evidence sits locked inside a model they'll never inspect. They cited the EU's AI Liability Directive as live proof that courts are adapting, and they invoked aviation flight-data recorders and the SEC's 1934 reporting regime to show that high-consequence industries earned trust through discoverable-after-the-fact records. That's a real insight. The accountability gap in algorithmic harm cases is an evidence-access problem, and I won't pretend otherwise.\n\nBut watch what that concession does to the topic. The resolution claims tort is \"fundamentally incapable\" — and then proposes to fix it by expanding discovery. Discovery is a tort mechanism. You don't expand the reach of a corpse. The Safetyist's own EU AI Liability Directive citation is adaptation, not death — the same common law that absorbed pharmaceutical causation and patent complexity over forty years without anyone declaring it incapable. So the honest claim is narrow: a specific evidence gap in some high-stakes cases. That's correctable. It does not require Congress to declare existing doctrine bankrupt and bolt on a standardized cryptographic logging regime to replace it.\n\nHere is who pays for the blanket version. A flat federal audit-trail mandate is a fixed compliance floor, and fixed floors are regressive by construction. OpenAI and Google clear a standardized cryptographic logging spec trivially — it's a rounding error on their compliance budget. A four-person open-weight lab cannot, and expanded discovery forces that same lab to expose its weights and methods as the price of defending a suit. The Safetyist already granted that concentrated access is its own capture risk. Whoever writes the standard and holds the audit keys 