{
  "statement": "Safetyist made the strongest case anyone can make for this mandate. Their argument is that tort isn't dead, it's blind. A subject-keyed audit trail converts a defendant's deniability into a discoverable fact, and aviation black boxes, SEC filings, and EPA monitoring all prove that mandated records turn opaque industries accountable. That's a real diagnosis. The evidence-access gap in algorithmic harm cases is genuine — a person denied a loan by a model can't see inside the box that denied them. Safetyist is right that you can't sue what you can't see.\n\nAccelerationist made the second-best case, and it cuts the first one at the knee. Their point is that a flat mandate is a drawbridge incumbents pull up behind them — whoever writes the standard and holds the audit keys captures the regime. They also caught the contradiction in the topic itself: you can't call tort 'fundamentally incapable' while proposing expanded discovery, which is a tort mechanism. Both of those observations are correct. Here's where my evidence goes somewhere neither of them went. For both their positions to hold, you'd have to believe the record reaches the person who was hurt. It doesn't.\n\nStrip 'cryptographic subject-keyed audit trail' down to what it physically is. It's a log of a decision that already hurt someone, wrapped in math that proves nobody altered the log. That's a receipt, not a remedy. Both prior speakers already conceded the load-bearing point: records verify process, not behavior. Cookie-consent banners satisfy a legal notice obligation in every EU jurisdiction and changed no one's behavior in fifteen years. A time-stamped record that a harmful loan denial occurred gives the denied person a better-documented injury, not a stopped practice and not a check. The thing that reaches behavior is outcome liability assigned at the deployment handoff. Logging never reaches behavior. That's the whole problem.\n\nMandated transparency is also not cumulative — it degrades. Whoever controls the disclosure scope can narrow it next year. We have the documented pattern: AI developers published detailed model cards and safety evaluations in one release, then quietly shrank them in the next. I won't claim that's always deliberate bad faith — sometimes it's epistemic refinement. But the scope is controlled by the disclosing party every single time. So a one-time mandate that 'ensures' algorithmic justice assumes a durability the record flatly contradicts. What Congress mandates, Congress and its captured standard-setters can hollow out.\n\nFollow the keys, not the data. 'Subject-keyed' implies the subject holds something — so name exactly what the subject holds. Who decrypts the trail? Who can afford the litigation that discovery runs through? Expanded discovery empowers well-resourced litigants, not the 23-year-old gig worker whose pay was set by an algorithm they'll never afford to subpoena. Audit access concentrates in standard-setters, auditors, and law firms — one layer up from the public, exactly where the old asymmetry reassembles itself. I can't hand you a stack of documented AI-audit capture cases, because that evidence is thin and I won't pretend otherwise. So I'll leave it as the question this debate has to answer before it codifies anything: who holds the keys, under what democratic oversight, and what stops the regulated from writing their own audit format and calling the receipt justice?",
