{
  "statement": "The four-person lab fielding a low-stakes tool should incur no logging obligation at all, and any mandate that says otherwise deserves to die. The burden must attach to the deployment context, not to headcount. Oversight intensity should scale with the stakes of the decision, not the size of the developer. I will not defend a flat federal floor — the Accelerationist is right that a uniform template applied to every developer entrenches incumbents, and the disclosure-standards approach carries exactly that vulnerability. The fix is to anchor the trigger in categories the law already defines: credit decisions under the Equal Credit Opportunity Act, employment decisions under Title VII, benefits decisions under due-process doctrine. 'High-stakes' is not an emergent capability I claim to measure. It is a domain of application Congress drew lines around decades ago.\n\nThe Department of Justice already wrote the proportionality rule the Accelerationist is asking for. The 2024 Evaluation of Corporate Compliance Programs judges a firm's obligations against its resources — a small operation is not held to the documentation standard of a Fortune 500. Port that standard here and the small-lab cost objection narrows to almost nothing. A four-person lab that never touches an ECOA credit decision logs nothing. The obligation tracks who profits from fielding the consequential decision, and at deployment scale that party is rarely the four-person upstream builder. No profit without accountability means the cost follows the revenue and the risk, not the org chart.\n\nNow the moat. The Accelerationist frames the mandate as the barrier — but look at who controls the information today. Without a discoverable record, the party that holds the data controls the outcome, and that party is the resourced incumbent who litigates a single plaintiff into the ground. The status quo is the moat. FTC v. Rite Aid in 2024 showed what the absence of a record produces: Rite Aid deployed facial-recognition that flagged shoppers as shoplifters, and the harmed parties had no internal trail to prove which decision injured them. The FTC reached it only with state enforcement muscle no private plaintiff commands. No mandate does not equalize the playing field. It freezes the asymmetry that already favors the incumbent.\n\nThe weight-exposure fear confuses two different things. Subject-keyed logging exposes the decision record — what outcome was rendered against whom and when — not the model internals. Civil procedure has solved trade-secret discovery for forty years through protective orders, in-camera review, and attorneys'-eyes-only designations. Pharmaceutical and chemical defendants surrender far more sensitive process data under those exact safeguards every year. The audit trail is keyed to the outcome, not the weights. A small lab is not asked to publish its model. It is asked to account for a decision it chose to field — a cost it currently externalizes onto the harmed subject.\n\nThe Skeptic calls the record a receipt, not a remedy. The receipt is not the remedy — it is the precondition for one. A strict-liability outcome regime cannot bite without the discoverable fact that establishes which actor's decision caused the harm. Here is the wedge the Accelerationist and Skeptic both walked past: locate all fault downstream and the upstream designer externalizes every distribution-shift failure onto the deployer. The audit trail is the only instrument that lets a court apportion between a design defect and a deployment misuse. Without it, a court cannot trace causation to either party — so it acquits both, and the harmed subject walks out with a documented injury and no defendant.",
