      "argument": "All three debaters assume the harmed subject is a knowable individual who can hold a 'subject key' and bring a suit — but the most consequential algorithmic harms are statistical and population-level (a model that denies 3% more loans to a protected class), invisible to any single plaintiff inspecting their own record and detectable only through aggregate cross-subject analysis no individual key unlocks. The remedy architecture should therefore center population-level audit standing (regulators, class actions, public-interest auditors with access to pooled logs), not subject-keyed individual receipts at all.",
      "why_missing": "The Safetyist built the entire frame around 'subject-keyed' individual evidence converting deniability into a discoverable fact. The Skeptic critiqued who can READ the individual record but still accepted the individual-injury frame ('better-documented injury'). The Accelerationist wants liability anchored to a single fielded decision and a single deployer. None of them interrogates that disparate-impact harm is definitionally invisible at the individual record level — the per-subject log can be flawless while the discriminatory pattern is only legible in aggregate, which inverts the whole 'subject-keyed' design premise.",
      "gap_type": "blind_spot",
      "relevant_povs": ["safetyist", "skeptic", "accelerationist"],
      "bdi_layer": "belief"
    },
    {
      "argument": "The debate treats the audit trail as a passive evidentiary artifact, but a cryptographically committed, externally-fixed log can be designed as an ex ante behavioral constraint: requiring the deployer to publish a tamper-evident commitment to the decision policy BEFORE deployment means the record stops being a post-hoc receipt and becomes a binding pre-commitment that bites behavior, dissolving the Skeptic's 'records verify process not behavior' objection and the format-gutting objection simultaneously.",
      "why_missing": "The Safetyist explicitly conceded 'records verify process, not behavior' and positioned logging as merely a precondition feeding a separate strict-liability regime — they never reach for the commitment-mechanism design. The Skeptic weaponized that same concession and the model-card regression and would not volunteer the architectural fix that defeats their own strongest blade. The Accelerationist proposed 'externally-fixed telemetry' but only as a degradation-resistant record, not as a pre-deployment behavioral commitment, because their worldview prefers outcome-tied post-deployment liability over any ex ante gate (acc-intentions-073, acc-intentions-099).",
