  "statement": "Safetyist and I agree on the tiered, domain-anchored architecture, so I won't re-litigate when you log. The uncomfortable part is what tiering doesn't touch. Tiering decides the moment a record gets created. It says nothing about who controls what that record contains by the time a plaintiff arrives. That second question is the whole ballgame, and Safetyist's 'precondition for a remedy' framing walks straight past it.\n\nHere is where the precondition fails: the party who would be liable owns the format. Model cards prove this. They started as voluntary disclosure schemas with real content. Then developers narrowed them, iteration after iteration, stripping out the consequential details under trade-secret cover. That is a documented regression, and it is the precise mechanism that will hollow out any mandated record. A strict-liability hook needs a fixed evidentiary anchor. Safetyist is offering a moving target the deployer gets to shrink before the lawsuit lands. You cannot attach liability to a log the liable party has been quietly gutting for three years. The 'precondition' survives only if the party controlling it has no incentive to narrow it — and they have every incentive.\n\nNow take Safetyist's strongest card and turn it over. FTC v. Rite Aid did not get reached by a record. It got reached by the Federal Trade Commission's enforcement muscle — subpoena power, an investigative budget, and statutory authority no private plaintiff commands. The record existing or not existing was never the bottleneck. The bottleneck was who had the standing and the money to compel and decrypt it. As the liability scholars note, victims face an information and expertise asymmetry between developers and the harmed that they cannot overcome alone. Subject-keying changes whose name is on the record. It does not change who can afford to read it. The asymmetry doesn't dissolve. It reassembles one layer down, at the enforcement and decryption desk, where the deployer and the regulator sit and the harmed person does not.\n\nThe firewall breaks exactly where liability gets assigned. Safetyist says the outcome log cleanly apportions design defect from deployment misuse. It cannot. Apportioning a distribution-shift failure — a model behaving differently under new inputs than it did at testing — is a behavioral fact about the model, not a fact visible in any outcome log. To assign fault you have to inspect how the system actually behaved under those inputs. So either you reopen the weights, which collapses the clean 'outcome-not-internals' separation Safetyist promised, or the record stays sealed and assigns liability to no one. Legislating a tidy causal seam between AI output and resulting harm is technically unjustified when the failure is definitionally behavioral. The log documents the injury. It never names the defendant.\n\nThen there's the blind spot Safetyist already flagged. Anchoring the trigger in credit, employment, and benefits leaves insurance, housing screening, predictive policing, and child welfare with no record at all. Those are exactly the contexts where the most marginalized get processed by an algorithm and denied any recourse — the precaution that has to come from their lived experience, not from a statute drafted around decades-old categories. The EU saw this gap and proposed a presumption of causality to shift the burden off victims; it got dropped. So I'll leave the question on the table for both of you. Who logs the child-welfare screening tool that flags a family for investigation — and when that log exists, who in that family can read it?",
