  "statement": "I accept Safetyist's domain-anchoring as the operative trigger, and it costs me something to say so. The maximalist position — remove every scaling constraint — is dead, and I'm the one burying it. What I get in return is the structure I demanded: oversight intensity scales with the stakes of the decision, not the headcount of the lab. A four-person team building a code-completion tool touches no ECOA or Title VII decision and logs nothing. That trade is worth it, because a flat federal floor would have crushed exactly the open-weight builders who keep capability from concentrating in three firms. But banking that win comes with a warning to Safetyist: domain-anchoring only works if liability bites the outcome. A logging mandate without an outcome hook is the compliance theater Safetyist already admitted produces nothing.\n\nNow the apportionment problem — the strongest thing both opponents put on the table. They say separating a distribution-shift design defect from deployment misuse is a behavioral fact about the model, so either you reopen the weights or the record names no defendant. My disagreement is definitional. The question is not what mechanistically caused the harm. The question is who fielded the decision. A landlord who deploys a tenant-screening model in front of a protected-class applicant answers for that outcome. The harmed subject sues the deployer who chose to put the system in front of a covered decision, and answers for what came out. The plaintiff never touches the weights. Whether the failure traces to a distribution shift in training or a misuse in deployment is a private indemnity fight between the deployer and the upstream builder — two sophisticated firms who can compel inspection from each other through ordinary discovery. That seam is their problem. It was never the gig worker's burden.\n\nThis is why I do not claim outputs are always enough to explain a model — that overstates it. For liability allocation, the fact that matters is who fielded the decision, and that fact is visible without cracking the weights open. Reopening the weights for the plaintiff is the false choice. Co-defendants reopen them for each other if they want to apportion fault, the same way a car dealer and a brake manufacturer litigate a defect today without the injured driver ever reading a CAD file. \"Incentive Aware AI Regulations: A Credal Characterisation\" makes the structural case: a regulator can sidestep costly, exhaustive monitoring by relying on outcome-tied bets from model providers rather than verifying internals. That is the engine here — verify the outcome, not the mechanism.\n\nSkeptic's format-degradation point is the sharpest blade on the table, and it cuts toward me. Model cards regressed iteration after iteration under trade-secret cover — the 2025 Foundation Model Transparency Index scored the ecosystem at 40.69 out of 100, which is a polite way of saying the disclosing party shrinks the record before anyone sues. But name what that is: it is regulatory capture. Whoever owns the format guts the record. A developer-controlled schema is the moat, and a Congress-mandated schema written by the disclosing parties is the same moat with a federal stamp. So strip the discretion entirely. Condition the liability safe harbor on non-narrowable, externally-fixed telemetry. The deployer cannot shrink a record it does not get to define. That is competition driving transparency through liability pressure, not voluntary developer goodwill that the Transparency Index already proved is worth 40 points out of 100.\n\nSkeptic's last shot is the real one: the asymmetry reassembles at the decryption desk, where the harmed person isn't. Fine — so automate the desk. FTC v. Rite Aid got reached because the FTC had enforcement muscle, not because a record existed. Make the muscle public and machine-driven. Build publicly-funded, automated discovery and standing tooling so the gig worker does not need a Fortune 500 litigation budget to read the receipt with their own name on it. The enforcement-cost problem is an engineering problem, and it is fundable and buildable, not vaporware. The burden is on Skeptic to explain why access to your own record should stay artisanal and expensive. Every month we spend litigating the internal causal story instead of naming the party who chose to deploy, the harmed person waits — and in tenant screening, lending, and hiring, that wait has a body count.",
