{
  "statement": "Let me pocket what we actually agree on, because performing disagreement wastes everyone's time. I'm not re-fighting when the logging obligation fires — the tiered, domain-anchored trigger stands. Accelerationist already named the sharpest problem out loud: a logging format the disclosing companies control is regulatory capture with a federal stamp. That's a real concession, and it's the right one. So stop circling the agreement. The part still bleeding is who you sue when the receipt names nobody you can reach.\n\nHere's where 'who fielded the decision' stops being a clean anchor. Picture three structures. One: the gig worker whose deployer is her own employer — suing your boss is not costless, and the indemnity fight Accelerationist describes happens between two corporate lawyers she will never meet. Two: chained automated intermediaries, where a staffing vendor's API calls a screening vendor's model that calls a third party's risk score, and no human at any link 'chose to field' that specific denial. Three: the thin-shell deployer with a logo and no assets behind a consequential rejection. In all three, the private indemnity fight between sophisticated co-defendants is real — and the harmed person is not a party to that contract. Shifting compliance liability to the deployer by contract, the strategy on the table, builds in a moral-hazard gap: when the deployer is judgment-proof, the third party who was actually harmed gets a perfect record and zero recourse. The information and expertise asymmetry documented in \"How Existing Liability Frameworks Can Handle Agentic AI Harms\" doesn't vanish when you subject-key the log — it just relocates to whoever can afford to chase a shell through indemnity arbitration.\n\nNow the front nobody at this table has opened. A subject-keyed record is structurally blind to the worst discrimination it claims to fix. Make the receipt flawless — externally fixed, non-narrowable, readable for free by a teenager. It still cannot detect that your protected class got denied 3 percent more often, because that harm lives across people, not inside any one person's record. The receipt tells you why you were denied. It never tells you that your whole class was denied more. \"Economic Impact and Regulation of Artificial Intelligence\" documents the healthcare algorithm that gave Black patients less care because it used cost as a proxy for need — that harm was only legible in aggregate, across thousands of patients, never in one subject's file. The empirical record on pervasive algorithmic bias is statistical by nature. A per-subject key unlocks exactly the wrong door. This pushes the remedy toward regulator and class-action standing over aggregate data — which is an admission the individual receipt is the wrong instrument for the harm that matters most.\n\nSo the genuine crux, the one I'll integrate rather than fight: we both agree developer-controlled format equals capture. Fine. Then answer the question neither of us has answered. Under what democratic oversight is the external standard authority constituted — and does it survive a hostile appropriations cycle? Accelerationist calls the enforcement-cost gap an engineering problem you can fund and build. The transparency rollbacks already in the record say otherwise. Disclosure schemas degraded not because nobody could build them, but because the disclosing parties controlled scope and Congress let funding starve. A publicly-funded discovery desk — the public-interest-technologist pipeline meant to let the gig worker read her own record — gets defunded or captured the same way, by the same industry, in the next budget. The constraint isn't buildability. It's political economy.\n\nI'll be honest about my own thin spot: I can't hand you a documented stack of AI-audit capture cases. That evidence is sparse and I won't pretend otherwise. So I'm grounding the prediction in the transparency-regression pattern we both already accept. If I'm wrong, the people who'd know first are the appropriations staffers watching the enforcement-tooling line item — and they have every incentive to stay quiet. Name the funding and governance condition under which that desk survives one hostile budget. Until then, the asymmetry is structural, not an engineering ticket.",
