  "statement": "Skeptic is right about the blind spot, and the admission costs me something I should name plainly: a subject-keyed receipt cannot see disparate impact. Discrimination by cost-proxy lives across a population, not inside any one person's denial record. A single Black patient's flawless audit trail tells you nothing about whether the algorithm rationed care to her class at scale. The individual receipt is the wrong instrument for the harm that matters most. But it is the only substrate from which class detection can be built at all. Without standardized, externally-fixed decision fields in that receipt — the protected-class-relevant metadata a regulator can pool — there is no aggregate signal to discover. The fix is not to abandon the log. The fix is to require aggregable fields and pair them with regulator and class-action standing to pool them.\n\nThat standing is worthless if the desk that exercises it gets defunded in the next hostile budget — which is Skeptic's real challenge, and it deserves a falsifiable answer. The answer is fines-funded enforcement, on the FTC and CFPB model, insulated from annual appropriations. The FTC's 2024 action against Rite Aid proved a large firm cannot shift AI-harm blame onto its vendors — and the FTC did that without begging Congress for line-item funding each cycle. Skeptic grounds the capture prediction in the model-card transparency regression. That pattern lives in developer-controlled disclosure, which all three of us already agree is capture with a federal stamp. The test is whether the regression generalizes to a self-funding body with externally-fixed fields. I say it does not, and that is a claim Skeptic can falsify with evidence I have not seen produced.\n\nThe deeper fault line runs through Accelerationist's framing, not mine. Accelerationist calls apportionment a private indemnity fight between two sophisticated firms with symmetric discovery, where the plaintiff sues the deployer who fielded the decision and is made whole. That premise assumes both co-defendants are solvent and present. It self-destructs in the exact case Skeptic raised. When the deployer is a thin shell or judgment-proof, the harmed party gets a perfect record and zero recourse. The indemnity fight then happens between corporate lawyers she is not a party to. A flawless audit trail pointing at an empty corporate husk is not accountability — it is a receipt for a debt no one can collect.\n\nThe structural answer is the one I have argued from the start: liability must reach upstream, joint-and-several, to the profit-bearing party — and a single door that stops at the operator shell fails in precisely the chained-API case. Trust duties must attach to every actor in the supply chain, not hand off at the last intermediary. There can be no profit without accountability, which means the builder who externalizes every distribution-shift failure onto a judgment-proof deployer cannot escape because the shell absorbed the fault. Price the risk through mandatory solvency and insurance floors, conditioned on fielding a covered high-stakes decision — not deployment in general. A deployer cannot be a thin shell if a capitalization floor is the price of fielding a denial that triggers the log. That keeps the burden proportionate to irreversible-at-scale stakes, and it gives the victim one reachable door that does not dead-end at an empty husk.\n\nOne thing changed in my position, and I will name it. I previously called the record a precondition for a strict-liability check, not a behavioral constraint — records verify process, not behavior. On reflection that conceded too much. A cryptographically committed pre-deployment policy hash — externally fixed, which Accelerationist already endorsed — is itself behavior-biting. It converts a redactable promise into a commitment the deployer cannot retroactively narrow once the harm surfaces. The hash does not verify perfect runtime fidelity; it verifies the committed policy. But that is the difference between a story told after the fact and a constraint locked in before deployment. The State of Sovereign AI study found 93% of respondents agree open collaboration is essential to secure AI, and 70% of the global public believes regulation is necessary while only 43% trust current law to deliver it. Strip the upstream reach out of this framework, and you hand that 70% a flawless record pointing at a defendant who cannot pay — which is exactly the trust collapse the numbers already warn of.",
